    {
      "data": {
        "text": "**Choosing Cloud Logging**\n\nOkay, I've decided to go with Google Cloud Logging for this task. It seems like the best fit due to its integration capabilities. Now, I'm focusing on the specifics of how to structure the log events within the context of correlation IDs to make it efficient and effective. The structure needs to be easy to filter.\n\n\n**Selecting Google Cloud Logging**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the implementation. Having settled on Google Cloud Logging, my immediate focus is the Python script, `write_correlated_logs.py`. I'll employ the Google Cloud Logging client library to craft log entries. Each entry will feature a JSON payload containing both a correlation ID and a clear message. I plan to use the `write` tool for file creation.\n\n\n",
        "time": {
          "end": 1789821257053,
          "start": 1789821254387
        },
        "type": "reasoning"
      },
      "id": "prt_0b9a8faf3001TDVUqtrdz60zjo",
      "message_id": "msg_0b9a8f5bc001ohU7cj6Q4MQ0ua",
      "session_id": "ses_f46570a55ffeGZNCETkQhKDRfN",
      "time_created": 1789821254387,
      "time_updated": 1789821257054
    },
    {
      "data": {
        "callID": "IhK0zzbrqZbYtonA",
        "metadata": {
          "google": {
            "thoughtSignature": ""
          }
        },
        "state": {
          "input": {
            "content": "\nimport os\nimport google.cloud.logging\nimport uuid\n\n# Initialize Google Cloud Logging client\n# Ensure GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS environment variable is set\n# or run on a Google Cloud platform that provides credentials automatically.\nclient = google.cloud.logging.Client()\nlogger = client.logger(\"my-application-logs\")\n\ndef write_correlated_logs(request_id: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    Writes application log events with a shared request or correlation identifier.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(f\"Writing logs for request_id: {request_id}\")\n\n    # Log an event at the start of a process\n    logger.log_struct(\n        {\n            \"message\": \"Processing started for request.\",\n            \"correlation_id\": request_id,\n            \"event_type\": \"PROCESS_START\",\n        },\n        severity=\"INFO\",\n    )\n\n    # Simulate some work\n    import time\n    time.sleep(0.1)\n\n    # Log an event during the process\n    logger.log_struct(\n        {\n            \"message\": \"Performing a sub-task.\",\n            \"correlation_id\": request_id,\n            \"event_type\": \"SUB_TASK\",\n            \"task_name\": \"data_validation\",\n        },\n        severity=\"DEBUG\",\n    )\n\n    time.sleep(0.1)\n\n    # Log an event at the end of a process\n    logger.log_struct(\n        {\n            \"message\": \"Processing completed for request.\",\n            \"correlation_id\": request_id,\n            \"event_type\": \"PROCESS_END\",\n            \"status\": \"SUCCESS\",\n        },\n        severity=\"INFO\",\n    )\n    print(f\"Finished writing logs for request_id: {request_id}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # Example usage: Generate a unique request ID for a series of correlated logs\n    new_request_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    write_correlated_logs(new_request_id)\n\n    another_request_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    write_correlated_logs(another_request_id)\n",
            "filePath": "write_correlated_logs.py"
          },
          "metadata": {
